Interviewer = I
Interviewee = P
Opening

I: Hi! Thank you so much for joining this short 10-minute interview today. We’re talking about a new club discovery website for students. Just share your genuine opinions, there are no right or wrong answers. Ready to start?
P: Sure, I’m good to go. Happy to help with your research.
I: Perfect, let’s begin.
Hypothesis 1: Smart Discovery (Filters)

I: First, imagine this website lets you filter clubs by interest, time commitment and difficulty level to find matching clubs quickly. Could you walk through your exact process if you used these filters right now?
P: I’d start by browsing general interest categories first to narrow down the types of clubs I’m curious about. Then I’d check difficulty to rule out clubs that require prior experience or advanced skills. Lastly, I’ll adjust the time filter to match my free time each week, so I only see realistic, doable options.
I: Among interest, time and difficulty, which filter is the most important to you? Please share your reason.
P: Interest is my top priority. I won’t stick to a club long-term if I’m not actually interested in the activity. Time and difficulty are secondary, because I can adjust my schedule or learn basic skills if I truly like the club’s theme.
I: What website design or details could make this filtering process feel effortless and smooth?
P: I prefer visual tags and one-click quick filters, instead of complicated drop-down lists. It would also be great if I could combine multiple filters at once and hide irrelevant results automatically, without extra clicking.
I: Even with these three core filters, are there still parts of the search that would make you feel unsure or confused?
P: Yeah, vague difficulty descriptions would confuse me a lot. Words like “moderate” have different meanings for everyone. Without extra explanations, I can’t tell if I’m qualified or not.
I: Outside of basic filters, what other key information do you need to trust that a club fits you well?
P: I want to know the overall group atmosphere, whether the club is casual or competitive, and if new members are welcomed and guided patiently.
I: Are there any extra filters you hope the website adds to speed up your club search?
P: I hope there’s a filter for membership fees and meeting frequency. Cost and how often the club meets are two big factors I always consider.
Hypothesis 2: Social-driven Features

I: Next scenario: the platform shows your friends’ activity, like who has joined or marked interest in a club. How much would this affect your willingness to check out a new club?
P: It would have a pretty big impact. Seeing people I know interested in a club makes it feel more reliable and less intimidating, so I’ll definitely click in to learn more.
I: Would you actively join a club just because your friends are in it? Why or why not?
P: Not entirely on its own. I’d check the club content first, but if my friends are there and the activity sounds okay, I’m much more likely to join. It’s nice to have familiar faces in a new group.
I: What kind of social features would make you want to use this club platform more frequently?
P: A trending club section based on student interests, plus a feature to see what clubs your classmates are currently exploring. It makes the whole platform feel more interactive.
I: Do you think seeing friends’ club activity would encourage you to try unique clubs you’d never pick alone?
P: Absolutely. I usually stick to my comfort zone, but friend recommendations can push me to try creative, sports or art clubs I’d normally overlook.
I: What other social functions do you want on a club platform to make joining more fun?
P: I’d love member feedback and short comment sections, plus a feature to see upcoming group events. It adds a social, lively vibe to the whole experience.
Hypothesis 3: Clear Club Information

I: Let’s move on. Every club profile clearly shows daily activities, meeting schedules, entry requirements and real member experiences. What’s the most essential information for you before joining?
P: Specific entry requirements and weekly activity content. I need to know what I need to prepare and what I’ll actually do as a member, instead of vague introductions.
I: Do you think current university club information is clear and well-structured? What’s missing or confusing?
P: Most club introductions are too superficial. They only write simple slogans and basic time slots, missing workload, member responsibilities and long-term plans, which makes it hard to compare options.
I: How can clear, organized club information help you make faster joining decisions?
P: I can compare multiple clubs side by side with clear info, save time on asking organizers repetitive questions, and quickly eliminate clubs that don’t meet my needs.
I: What details about club culture and community should be permanently added to every profile?
P: I think leadership style, new member onboarding arrangements, and whether the club holds relaxed social events are super important cultural details to display.
I: What profile details would make you fully certain a club is a perfect match for you?
P: Transparent time arrangements, clear role expectations, inclusive community culture, and real previews of regular club activities.
Hypothesis 4: Easy Joining Process

I: Last scenario: the website provides a simple step-by-step online application, and you can finish joining a club in just a few minutes. How would this easy process change your joining motivation?
P: It would greatly increase my motivation. Complicated, time-consuming application processes always make me lose interest and give up halfway. A quick process lowers the threshold to try new clubs.
I: For this ideal simple joining flow, what is the minimum number of steps and basic information you think is necessary?
P: Around 2–3 simple steps is perfect. Basic info like name, student email and a short answer about your interest in the club is enough; no redundant questions.
I: Are there any traditional joining procedures you hope to remove completely, such as paperwork or long review waiting times?
P: I really want to cancel offline paper forms and manual long review periods. Instant online submission and fast approval are much more student-friendly.
I: Even if the joining process is designed to be simple, what factors might still stop you from finishing the application?
P: If the form asks for overly private information, or the page has confusing navigation. Small usability issues will make me exit the page directly.
I: How would you design a perfect, quick and pleasant joining experience for students?
P: Clean interface, concise forms, real-time progress tips, and an immediate success notification after submission. The whole process should feel effortless and efficient.
Closing

I: Alright, that’s all of our planned questions today. Thank you so much for sharing your detailed and personal thoughts on club filtering, social functions, profile information and sign-up processes. Your feedback is really valuable.
P: No problem at all, it was an interesting topic to talk about. I’m glad I could help.
I: Really appreciate your time and honest sharing. Before we end, do you have any extra thoughts or suggestions about finding and joining student clubs that we didn’t cover?
P: Not for me, I think we’ve covered all the key points I care about.
I: Great. Thanks again for participating in the interview. Have a wonderful day!
P: You too, take care!


